  "description": "APPLICATION FOR A&nbsp;LAWFUL DEVELOPMENT CERTIFICATE IN RESPECT OF THE PROPOSED PARTIAL CONVERSION OF EXISTING DETACHED GARAGE INTO HABITABLE ROOM/STUDIO WITH ENSUITE FOR INCIDENTAL USE, NEW FENESTRATION TO THE FRONT AND REAR FACADES AND INSTALLATION OF RAISED DECKING",
  "epc_number": null,
  "ward": "West Barnes",
  "last_updated": "2026-10-06T12:26:58.789Z",
  "street_name": "WOODLANDS",
  "decision_conditions": [
    "<p style=\"text-align: justify; \"><span style=\"line-height: 107%;\"><u><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Erection of an outbuilding (Class E) </span></u><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">- The operations would consist of a raised patio/decking for purposes incidental to that of the main house within the meaning of Class E of Part 1 of\r\nSchedule 2 to the Town &amp; Country Planning (General Permitted Development)\r\n(England) Order 2015 \"the Order\" and would comply with the\r\ndevelopment tolerances set out in Class E. The operation would therefore have\r\nbeen granted planning permission by Article 3 of the Order.</span></span></p>",
    "&lt;p class=&quot;MsoNormal&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;Non-material alteration&lt;/u&gt; - The insertion of windows and door to existing outbuilding would consist of a non-material alteration\r\nto the external appearance of the building within the meaning of\r\nSection 55 (2) Town &amp;amp; Country Planning Act 1990 and Town &amp;amp; Country\r\nPlanning (General Permitted Development) (England) Order 2015 &quot;the\r\nOrder&quot;&amp;nbsp; and would therefore not\r\nconstitute development as defined in Section 55 (2) and the relevant parts of\r\nthe Act.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;",
    "&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;Not development&lt;/u&gt; - The use of the existing outbuilding for purposes incidental to the main dwellinghouse do not constitute development as defined in Section 55 of the\r\nTown &amp;amp; Country Planning Act 1990 (as amended). Therefore no planning\r\npermission would be required.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;"
  ],
